    "result_text": "The acceptance suite is the entire test set. All tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature for `POST /api/owners`. The Cp10 duplicate-household rejection (with the `sharesHousehold` opt-out) was already present; the missing piece was assigning and returning a stable shared `householdId`.\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `householdId` (readOnly string) property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it into `OwnerDto`.\n- **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `household_id` column (tests run with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema is script-driven).\n- **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `householdIdFor(lastName, address)`, which derives the id deterministically as upper-case hex of SHA-256 over the *collapsed* last name + address (the same normalization used by the existing duplicate-household check), and assigns it in `addOwner`.\n\nBecause the id is a pure function of the household's last name and address, every owner in the same household \u2014 the original owner and any later joiners created with `sharesHousehold: true` \u2014 receives the identical, non-blank identifier without needing to read any other owner's value. That satisfies Cp11 (joiners share the same `householdId`) while Cp10's rejection/opt-in behavior is unchanged.\n\nAll 21 tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
